**Read:** the full [CPC proportionality calibration — Owen, Mulholland and Guthrie](../sources/CPC-Proportionality-Calibration-Owen-Mulholland-Guthrie.lingua#r_7XKKJ1Q7HNDS2BEPSHZDJMTKVE) manuscript and [Calibration of aerosol instruments — Chen, Fletcher and Cheng](../sources/Aerosol-Instrument-Calibration-Chen-Fletcher-Cheng.lingua#r_03PDE8353KQ9HQKJNAHRH99NPP), calibration design, flow and CPC sections.

Reconstruct the chain from a characterized aerosol to reference current, sampled flow, corrected count and calibrated concentration. Track charge assumptions, splitter orientation, detector response and gas-volume basis. Separate what the paper measured from what your reconstruction assumes.

Reproduce a bounded published calculation when the inputs are available, preserving the source version and any transcription uncertainty. If the original observations are unavailable, create a clearly labelled teaching dataset and test the inference without claiming experimental reproduction. Compare proportional, offset and coincidence-affected response models.

Calculate a swap-based splitter estimate, a dilution-ratio sequence and an uncertainty budget. Challenge the result with a changed aerosol size, a drifting diluter and an uncertain background. Demonstrate what the reference comparison fixes and what a proportionality test leaves undetermined.

**Result to explain:** A defensible calibration relation over stated conditions, residual checks and uncertainty, plus a precise account of what additional evidence would be needed to transfer it to another production aerosol. A small fit residual alone is not the final argument.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
